WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.367 --> 00:00:02.668
-Our first guest tonight
is an Emmy-nominated actor

00:00:02.668 --> 00:00:05.138
you know from shows like
"Heroes" and "Gilmore Girls."

00:00:05.138 --> 00:00:06.839
He stars in the
critically-acclaimed series

00:00:06.839 --> 00:00:09.476
"This Is Us."
The sixth and final season

00:00:09.476 --> 00:00:11.511
airs Tuesday nights here on NBC.

00:00:11.511 --> 00:00:12.679
Let's a take look.

00:00:12.679 --> 00:00:16.049
-Babe, it ain't worth it.
[Smooches] Alright?

00:00:16.049 --> 00:00:19.219
I mean, the kids they --
they barely knew her.

00:00:19.219 --> 00:00:20.953
I mean, when was the last time
she visited?

00:00:20.953 --> 00:00:22.855
They were what?
Like, a year old?

00:00:22.855 --> 00:00:24.790
Yeah, but they talk to her
on the phone.

00:00:24.790 --> 00:00:26.992
And that's all she's been
for years --

00:00:26.992 --> 00:00:29.995
just a voice on the other end
of a telephone.

00:00:29.995 --> 00:00:34.767
Look, babe, knowing my mom,
I got a funeral to plan

00:00:34.767 --> 00:00:38.738
and a lifetime of loose ends
to tie up.

00:00:38.738 --> 00:00:42.041
Look, it's just -- it's better
if I do this alone.

00:00:42.041 --> 00:00:44.611
-Please welcome to back to the
show Milo Ventimiglia.

00:00:44.611 --> 00:00:45.912
Hello, Milo.

00:00:45.912 --> 00:00:46.912
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:46.912 --> 00:00:48.180
-What's happening, Seth?

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:49.816
What's going on, man?
Good to see you.

00:00:49.816 --> 00:00:51.351
-It's great to see you.

00:00:51.351 --> 00:00:52.786
Congratulations.

00:00:52.786 --> 00:00:55.788
It's very hard to do a TV show
that ends on its own terms.

00:00:55.788 --> 00:00:58.925
That must be a very satisfying
thing to be a part of.

00:00:58.925 --> 00:01:01.261
-Very satisfying thing,
although we're not quite

00:01:01.261 --> 00:01:03.396
there at the end. We're right in
the thick of season six.

00:01:03.396 --> 00:01:06.766
But it's nice to know that we've
had the runway to land

00:01:06.766 --> 00:01:09.368
the plane the way we want to
land the plane,

00:01:09.368 --> 00:01:12.405
versus other shows where it just
kind of, like, crashes.

00:01:12.405 --> 00:01:14.573
-[ Laughs ]
Or you're in the air,

00:01:14.573 --> 00:01:16.575
and then the network calls
and is like, "We need you.

00:01:16.575 --> 00:01:18.011
We're not going to let you out
of the plane anymore."

00:01:18.011 --> 00:01:19.812
-Dude, or better yet -- or
better yet, they're like,

00:01:19.812 --> 00:01:21.181
"Yeah, you're not going to land
the plane.

00:01:21.181 --> 00:01:22.581
You're going to continue to fly.

00:01:22.581 --> 00:01:25.017
You're gonna circle the runway
for years and years.

00:01:25.017 --> 00:01:28.288
-We talked to some of your
castmates about who will be

00:01:28.288 --> 00:01:31.257
the biggest crier on the set
the last day.

00:01:31.257 --> 00:01:33.226
I know you mentioned you're only
about half way there.

00:01:33.226 --> 00:01:35.328
How are you feeling emotionally?

00:01:35.328 --> 00:01:37.363
-Emotionally, I'm pretty good.

00:01:37.363 --> 00:01:38.931
I think I feel pretty solid.

00:01:38.931 --> 00:01:41.568
I've got a lot of resolve about
showing up to work every day,

00:01:41.568 --> 00:01:43.669
spending time with the crew.

00:01:43.669 --> 00:01:45.305
We got a great crew.

00:01:45.305 --> 00:01:49.742
But I'm sure those last moments,

00:01:49.742 --> 00:01:51.177
everyone's going to be
in tears.

00:01:51.177 --> 00:01:52.211
Everyone's going to be crying.

00:01:52.211 --> 00:01:54.280
Us in production.
The audience --

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:56.815
I think we've established
the audience will be crying.

00:01:56.815 --> 00:01:58.584
But us in production,

00:01:58.584 --> 00:02:01.053
I have a feeling that we'll be
emotional.

00:02:01.053 --> 00:02:03.489
We'll have a hard time
letting go.

00:02:03.489 --> 00:02:04.624
I mean, you know this.
you're on the show

00:02:04.624 --> 00:02:07.259
for so many years,
and all of a sudden,

00:02:07.259 --> 00:02:10.029
you remember that first moment
and then it's done,

00:02:10.029 --> 00:02:14.100
and then you're like, "Oh, wait,
I may not see these people again

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:18.370
or every day or maybe in passing
or social media or whatever."

00:02:18.370 --> 00:02:20.707
So it's bittersweet.

00:02:20.707 --> 00:02:23.976
-I really do hope the audience
cries at the finale of the show.

00:02:23.976 --> 00:02:27.680
That would be the great final
twist if the last --

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:31.117
if the last episode of the show
just wasn't emotional at all.

00:02:31.117 --> 00:02:33.419
-Oh, no. it's like a "Seinfeld"
episode or something.

00:02:33.419 --> 00:02:34.787
All of a sudden, like,
people are just like,

00:02:34.787 --> 00:02:36.689
"What are all these, like,
[imitates rim shot] jokes?"

00:02:36.689 --> 00:02:38.123
Like, "What is happening?
I don't understand this."

00:02:38.123 --> 00:02:41.460
-Super jokey.
The last episode's super hacky.

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:43.162
-Totally.
-You also got to be --

00:02:43.162 --> 00:02:44.730
-You know, we heard --
by the clip, you know,

00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:46.432
most of the work you did,
obviously, on the show

00:02:46.432 --> 00:02:49.435
was with Mandy. And, you know,
it was a bigger cast than that,

00:02:49.435 --> 00:02:52.605
but because of the different
times the show takes place,

00:02:52.605 --> 00:02:55.240
you don't interact, obviously.
But you did go behind the camera

00:02:55.240 --> 00:02:56.442
and direct some episodes.

00:02:56.442 --> 00:02:59.912
How was it to direct
your other cast mates?

00:02:59.912 --> 00:03:02.114
-It was funny.
sometimes, you know,

00:03:02.114 --> 00:03:04.450
because I don't act with them
on a regular basis,

00:03:04.450 --> 00:03:08.354
I'm not on set with them,
so at first, my direction

00:03:08.354 --> 00:03:10.356
is met with a little kind of
side eye,

00:03:10.356 --> 00:03:11.690
like kind of looking at me,

00:03:11.690 --> 00:03:13.292
like we don't have
that trust yet.

00:03:13.292 --> 00:03:15.795
Like, I mean, we could knock out
a press conference together

00:03:15.795 --> 00:03:17.163
no sweat.

00:03:17.163 --> 00:03:21.633
But it's been a pleasure to be
creative with the cast,

00:03:21.633 --> 00:03:25.337
to actually be there,
get that front row seat

00:03:25.337 --> 00:03:27.273
into what they're doing
week in and week out.

00:03:27.273 --> 00:03:30.676
And, you know, I got to say,
I mean, they're all so talented,

00:03:30.676 --> 00:03:34.713
and the words are there,
and the crew is well-oiled.

00:03:34.713 --> 00:03:36.950
The whole process of it
is very smooth,

00:03:36.950 --> 00:03:39.785
especially for, you know,
us directors.

00:03:39.785 --> 00:03:41.386
There's not a whole lot
we have to do

00:03:41.386 --> 00:03:43.789
other than pay attention.

00:03:43.789 --> 00:03:46.158
Kind of give like a bit of
a Tom Hanks,

00:03:46.158 --> 00:03:48.861
you know, faster, slower.

00:03:48.861 --> 00:03:50.095
I forgot whatever he said.

00:03:50.095 --> 00:03:52.965
He goes, you know, faster,
slower, louder softer --

00:03:52.965 --> 00:03:54.500
whatever it is, he gives,
like --

00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:56.302
there's like four directions
you need to give as a director.

00:03:56.302 --> 00:03:58.471
-Maybe this is why you were
getting side eye on set.

00:03:58.471 --> 00:04:00.473
[ Laughter ]

00:04:00.473 --> 00:04:01.741
-Probably, yeah.

00:04:01.741 --> 00:04:03.443
Like, "Mi, can you give us
a little more, please?

00:04:03.443 --> 00:04:04.543
Mi, please?"

00:04:04.543 --> 00:04:05.878
-Well, one thing you've
accomplished,

00:04:05.878 --> 00:04:07.480
and I'm pretty sure they can't
take these away

00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:09.515
once you have them,
although I actually think

00:04:09.515 --> 00:04:11.050
a lot of people
have gotten them taken away.

00:04:11.050 --> 00:04:12.818
-[ Laughs ]
-For, like, bad behavior.

00:04:12.818 --> 00:04:13.919
-Well -- Well --
-But you got a star

00:04:13.919 --> 00:04:15.054
on the Hollywood Walk of Fame.
-I did.

00:04:15.054 --> 00:04:16.622
-I think yours is safe, yeah.

00:04:16.622 --> 00:04:17.923
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:17.923 --> 00:04:19.925
-Thanks, man. I mean,
look, I think you're right.

00:04:19.925 --> 00:04:24.230
I think they can lobby to have
some taken away

00:04:24.230 --> 00:04:25.998
because they continually
get destroyed.

00:04:25.998 --> 00:04:28.034
You might have -- You know,
the previous conversation.

00:04:28.034 --> 00:04:30.469
I think that star might have
been destroyed a couple times.

00:04:30.469 --> 00:04:32.438
-Yeah.
-But, yeah.

00:04:32.438 --> 00:04:36.109
I'm hoping that mine will stay
there for a while.

00:04:36.109 --> 00:04:38.210
I'm hoping it inspires
young artists.

00:04:38.210 --> 00:04:40.079
Yeah, I'm right there next to
Mandy Moore, my TV wife.

00:04:40.079 --> 00:04:41.313
-I was going to say,
when I looked at it, I'm like,

00:04:41.313 --> 00:04:42.915
"It's very unclear
who you are gesturing to,

00:04:42.915 --> 00:04:44.283
but that is very nice."
You, uh --

00:04:44.283 --> 00:04:45.718
I was like, "Do you --
Were you--

00:04:45.718 --> 00:04:47.286
Are you like, 'I got two?'"

00:04:47.286 --> 00:04:48.387
[ Laughter ]

00:04:48.387 --> 00:04:49.755
-Yeah, well, the funny thing
was --

00:04:49.755 --> 00:04:51.357
-And this is my actor one,
and that's my director one.

00:04:51.357 --> 00:04:52.892
-[ Laughs ]
-Yeah, totally. I know.

00:04:52.892 --> 00:04:54.594
Wouldn't that be wild?
Actually, there are --

00:04:54.594 --> 00:04:55.895
I feel like there are
some people, though,

00:04:55.895 --> 00:04:58.297
that do have, because there's
the movie symbol,

00:04:58.297 --> 00:05:00.666
then there's the TV symbol,
then there's the radio symbol.

00:05:00.666 --> 00:05:02.535
I feel there are some people
that do have a couple.

00:05:02.535 --> 00:05:05.171
-That seems a little --
that seems like overdoing it.

00:05:05.171 --> 00:05:08.407
I think if you have more than
one star, that's bad behavior.

00:05:08.407 --> 00:05:12.311
-So you grew up out in
California.

00:05:12.311 --> 00:05:15.314
I guess in -- Was it in Anaheim
is where you grew up?

00:05:15.314 --> 00:05:20.219
-Yeah. I guess kind of, like,
right there-ish,

00:05:20.219 --> 00:05:21.353
right around there.
-Thank you.

00:05:21.353 --> 00:05:22.354
And thank you
for bringing a map.

00:05:22.354 --> 00:05:23.922
I know we gave you very --

00:05:23.922 --> 00:05:26.258
We gave you very short notice
that we would like a map,

00:05:26.258 --> 00:05:27.259
and you really pulled it off.

00:05:27.259 --> 00:05:29.061
-I did some art direction.

00:05:29.061 --> 00:05:30.496
I called my friend,
Aaron Draplin.

00:05:30.496 --> 00:05:32.498
I was like, "Hey, man, do you
have that California poster?

00:05:32.498 --> 00:05:34.367
Please, please.
I got to put it behind me

00:05:34.367 --> 00:05:38.004
to let everybody know
that I'm not in New York."

00:05:38.004 --> 00:05:39.271
Yeah, I grew up in Anaheim.

00:05:39.271 --> 00:05:41.140
I went to Disneyland
all the time.

00:05:41.140 --> 00:05:45.578
I grew up a total normal kid
having fun, doing dumb stuff

00:05:45.578 --> 00:05:47.580
that kids do.

00:05:47.580 --> 00:05:49.248
And then I was also kind of
acting,

00:05:49.248 --> 00:05:51.917
so it was a good place
to grow up.

00:05:51.917 --> 00:05:54.420
-So you had an early --
you know, your career

00:05:54.420 --> 00:05:56.622
got started, I guess,
after college really,

00:05:56.622 --> 00:06:00.292
but as a young actor,
you auditioned for a movie

00:06:00.292 --> 00:06:01.728
and you got pretty close.

00:06:01.728 --> 00:06:04.429
-You're talking about
"Radio Flyer?"

00:06:04.429 --> 00:06:06.098
-"Radio Flyer."

00:06:06.098 --> 00:06:07.666
-I think so. Or you're like,
"No.

00:06:07.666 --> 00:06:08.801
I'm going to go with whatever he
says."

00:06:08.801 --> 00:06:09.969
You could have said
"Terminator."

00:06:09.969 --> 00:06:11.937
I tried out for "Terminator."
Yeah, yeah.

00:06:11.937 --> 00:06:14.640
-Yeah, I heard it came down to
you or Schwarzenegger.

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:17.476
It was going to be a very,
different movie

00:06:17.476 --> 00:06:20.613
where it was a very young --
a young Terminator.

00:06:20.613 --> 00:06:22.815
-Can you imagine, like,
12-year-old Milo, like,

00:06:22.815 --> 00:06:24.951
running through --
Well, not "Terminator."

00:06:24.951 --> 00:06:26.251
Sorry, I went to "Predator."

00:06:26.251 --> 00:06:29.521
Being chased in a Colombian
jungle by some beast?

00:06:29.521 --> 00:06:31.390
I'm like, "No, I'm a kid from
Orange County!"

00:06:31.390 --> 00:06:32.924
[ Laughter ]

00:06:32.924 --> 00:06:37.696
-You also are getting back
together with your gang --

00:06:37.696 --> 00:06:40.366
"Gilmore Girls" gang,
Amy Sherman-Palladino.

00:06:40.366 --> 00:06:42.134
-You're in the next season of --
-Yes, sir.

00:06:42.134 --> 00:06:43.402
-Next season of "Maisel."

00:06:43.402 --> 00:06:44.703
This is very exciting.
-Yup.

00:06:44.703 --> 00:06:45.938
-Look at you.

00:06:45.938 --> 00:06:47.573
-And I feel like --
-Yeah, there she is.

00:06:47.573 --> 00:06:49.475
There's Midge walking up
a nice hill,

00:06:49.475 --> 00:06:50.642
and me in a turtle neck.

00:06:50.642 --> 00:06:52.044
I mean, who doesn't want to
watch that?

00:06:52.044 --> 00:06:54.447
-I want to say, I was like,
"Oh, I hope you're proud of

00:06:54.447 --> 00:06:56.081
your turtle neck,"
and based on this pose,

00:06:56.081 --> 00:06:57.516
you're very proud
of your turtle neck.

00:06:57.516 --> 00:06:58.717
-Oh, yeah.

00:06:58.717 --> 00:07:01.053
I mean, that is a
Proud-of-My-Turtle-Neck stance

00:07:01.053 --> 00:07:02.788
right there --
hands on hips.

00:07:02.788 --> 00:07:06.558
-Yeah.
-You know, like '80s dad bod

00:07:06.558 --> 00:07:09.261
because I can't hide jack belly
just sticking out there.

00:07:09.261 --> 00:07:10.830
-Yeah, this is, like --

00:07:10.830 --> 00:07:13.532
I feel like this pose
was in every catalog

00:07:13.532 --> 00:07:14.600
my dad ever got.

00:07:14.600 --> 00:07:17.302
[ Laughter ]

00:07:17.302 --> 00:07:19.805
-Sears Roebuck. Sears Roebuck.
-Yeah, very Roebuck.

00:07:19.805 --> 00:07:23.442
That must be exciting to work
with Amy again.

00:07:23.442 --> 00:07:26.578
-It's a blast. I mean, you know,
she and Dan Palladino,

00:07:26.578 --> 00:07:30.049
her husband, and they were just
so good to me

00:07:30.049 --> 00:07:31.517
back in the "Gilmore" days.

00:07:31.517 --> 00:07:33.285
I mean, I don't know if you ever
knew, but they were planning on

00:07:33.285 --> 00:07:36.154
spinning Jess off
on to his own show

00:07:36.154 --> 00:07:39.091
when they sent him off to
California to meet his father,

00:07:39.091 --> 00:07:41.794
and that just didn't work
for whatever reason.

00:07:41.794 --> 00:07:43.095
But they've been very good to
me.

00:07:43.095 --> 00:07:44.296
They've kept up with me.

00:07:44.296 --> 00:07:47.566
And Amy actually came out to
California

00:07:47.566 --> 00:07:50.102
for the star ceremony.
She spoke at it.

00:07:50.102 --> 00:07:54.807
So she is one of those people
that whenever she calls,

00:07:54.807 --> 00:07:57.009
whenever she says, "Hey, Mi,
I need you for this,"

00:07:57.009 --> 00:07:58.710
I'm always going to be there.

00:07:58.710 --> 00:08:01.680
They're wonderful people.
They're crazy talented.

00:08:01.680 --> 00:08:03.516
And also, like, Rachel.
She's a pal of mine.

00:08:03.516 --> 00:08:06.652
So to be on camera, you know,
playing the scenes

00:08:06.652 --> 00:08:09.154
that we were playing -- I mean,
she's just --

00:08:09.154 --> 00:08:10.289
She's a lot of fun.
She's a lot of fun.

00:08:10.289 --> 00:08:11.690
So it's an easy group
to be around.

00:08:11.690 --> 00:08:13.192
-Well, that's great.
I can't wait to see it,

00:08:13.192 --> 00:08:14.493
but I also want to ask
about this.

00:08:14.493 --> 00:08:15.928
So, here you are.

00:08:15.928 --> 00:08:18.197
You're at the Indy 500 here,
yes?

00:08:18.197 --> 00:08:20.766
-[ Laughs ] Yeah.
-And you are -- That's you.

00:08:20.766 --> 00:08:22.701
-That's me.
-Waving the green flag.

00:08:22.701 --> 00:08:25.937
And this is -- you just snuck
past security, right?

00:08:25.937 --> 00:08:27.339
This is -- you just crawled up?

00:08:27.339 --> 00:08:29.909
-Oh, yeah. I basically bowled
past a bunch of people,

00:08:29.909 --> 00:08:31.344
climbed the stairs.
You know what I did, too?

00:08:31.344 --> 00:08:32.945
I even brought Russ.
I brought my buddy, Russ.

00:08:32.945 --> 00:08:34.280
Russ is in the back
with a jean jacket.

00:08:34.280 --> 00:08:35.714
He's holding --
If you zoom in on there,

00:08:35.714 --> 00:08:37.916
he's holding up his phone.

00:08:37.916 --> 00:08:39.952
Yeah, go right, right, right.
-Oh, yeah.

00:08:39.952 --> 00:08:41.854
Yeah, there's Russ in the back
holding his phone.

00:08:41.854 --> 00:08:43.722
No, no, keep going.
Keep going, keep going.

00:08:43.722 --> 00:08:45.358
Right there. That's Russ.
-Yeah.

00:08:45.358 --> 00:08:47.326
-That's my producing partner,
Russ.

00:08:47.326 --> 00:08:49.228
I snuck him up there.
-That's great.

00:08:49.228 --> 00:08:51.563
-Grabbed the flag from the
gentleman who usually

00:08:51.563 --> 00:08:52.564
waves the green flag.

00:08:52.564 --> 00:08:54.433
His name is Aaron.
-Yeah.

00:08:54.433 --> 00:08:56.668
-And I just waved the heck out
of that thing. It was a blast.

00:08:56.668 --> 00:08:58.270
-And none of the cars were
ready, and they were all like,

00:08:58.270 --> 00:08:59.438
"Oh, no, no, no, no, no!"

00:08:59.438 --> 00:09:01.273
-Oh, what is it?

00:09:01.273 --> 00:09:02.474
They were ready.
They were ready.

00:09:02.474 --> 00:09:06.011
-Can't imagine a louder honor
than that one.

00:09:06.011 --> 00:09:08.180
-I had ear plugs in.

00:09:08.180 --> 00:09:09.681
It was loud.
It was loud.

00:09:09.681 --> 00:09:12.117
I mean, when those cars are
just racing underneath you,

00:09:12.117 --> 00:09:13.752
just ripping around that track.

00:09:13.752 --> 00:09:15.454
I mean, I actually --

00:09:15.454 --> 00:09:17.823
so my buddy, Conor Daly,
he's a racing driver.

00:09:17.823 --> 00:09:19.492
He was the one that brought us
out there

00:09:19.492 --> 00:09:20.860
and said, "Hey, you should
come out."

00:09:20.860 --> 00:09:23.495
But then before that,
the day before, they were like,

00:09:23.495 --> 00:09:26.398
"Do you want to get into
a race car with Mario Andretti

00:09:26.398 --> 00:09:28.667
and have Mario Andretti
take you around the track?"

00:09:28.667 --> 00:09:30.469
And I was like, "Yes."

00:09:30.469 --> 00:09:31.970
That was my first thought.

00:09:31.970 --> 00:09:37.576
My second thought was, "Isn't
he, like, late 70s, early 80s?"

00:09:37.576 --> 00:09:39.178
Yeah, sure. Why not?
Let's do it.

00:09:39.178 --> 00:09:40.312
Cool, let's do it.

00:09:40.312 --> 00:09:41.313
[ Laughter ]

00:09:41.313 --> 00:09:42.681
-Yeah, I think
if there's ever --

00:09:42.681 --> 00:09:44.683
I mean, I wouldn't get -- you
know, most 80-year-olds,

00:09:44.683 --> 00:09:46.351
I'd say no, but I think
Mario Andretti

00:09:46.351 --> 00:09:48.287
would probably be a safe bet.
-Yeah, totally.

00:09:48.287 --> 00:09:49.789
-Hey, thanks so much
for being here.

00:09:49.789 --> 00:09:52.090
Congrats on the sixth
and final season.

00:09:52.090 --> 00:09:54.026
We really appreciate your time.

00:09:54.026 --> 00:09:56.428
-Thanks, man.
Always great to see you, Seth.

00:09:56.428 --> 00:09:57.730
-Milo Ventimiglia,
everybody.

